emails.content:
Hi Mara,

I’ve now heard back from Northway Freight regarding the claim for the pallet of gauge housings and telemetry batteries that was due at the Calder depot on 14 March but did not arrive until 22 March.

They have accepted that the eight-day delay fell outside the three-day service in consignment note NF-784193. They have also agreed that the pallet was incorrectly held at the Preston hub from 15 to 20 March, rather than being transferred to the Calder route as their tracking updates originally suggested.

Their revised offer is £1,486.70, broken down as follows:

• £612.00 refund of the priority freight charge;
• £524.70 for the replacement hire of the temporary telemetry units;
• £350.00 as a contribution towards the additional technician visit on 19 March.

This is an improvement on the £962.00 offer they sent on 9 April, but it is still £438.30 below the £1,925.00 total in our claim schedule dated 2 April. They have again declined the remaining call-out cost, saying the second vehicle and overtime were an operational choice rather than a direct consequence of the delay.

I pushed back on that point because your incident note, “Calder Gauge Deployment Delay – 19 March,” clearly explains why two technicians were required to keep the temporary sensor operating while the damaged battery enclosure was made safe. I also reminded them that the original delivery booking listed the equipment as time-critical for flood-monitoring continuity. They have asked for one final item before reconsidering: a copy of the duty rota or job allocation showing that the 19 March visit was additional to the planned maintenance schedule.

Could you send me either the relevant page from the March field rota or an email from Tom confirming the extra attendance? Please redact personal mobile numbers if they appear. If I can forward that by 4 p.m. on Friday, 26 April, Northway have said their claims manager will issue a final decision by 3 May.

For reference, I now have the following documents in the claim file:

• consignment note NF-784193;
• Northway tracking history, 13–22 March;
• delivery receipt signed at 10:42 on 22 March;
• supplier invoice RW-11847 for £8,760.00;
• telemetry hire invoice HST-4408 for £524.70;
• your incident note dated 20 March;
• claim schedule dated 2 April; and
• Northway’s revised offer letter dated 23 April.

Unless you think otherwise, I recommend that we do not accept the £1,486.70 yet. The offer remains open until 8 May, so there is enough time to provide the rota evidence and pursue the outstanding £438.30 without risking the amount already conceded. Northway have confirmed in writing that supplying further evidence will not withdraw the current offer.

Also, their letter refers to the contents as “general electrical equipment.” I’ll correct that in my response to “river-gauge telemetry equipment and sealed battery housings,” as described on the booking form. That distinction may help reinforce why the delivery date mattered.

Thanks again for tracking down the temporary-unit paperwork last week. Send the rota extract when you have a moment, and I’ll submit the complete response under claim reference CLM-55927.

Best,

Leanne Whitaker

